.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
.TH FREECUPL 1 2026-11-20 "FreeCUPL 2.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
freecupl \- CUPL compatible PLD compiler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B freecupl
.RI [ \-flags ]
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ library ]
.RI [ device ]
.IR file [ .pld ]
.br
.B freecupl
.B \-devices
.br
.B freecupl
.B \-V
.br
.B freecupl serve
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-browser ]
.RI [ directory ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B freecupl
compiles a CUPL source file into a JEDEC fuse file for a programmable
logic device, with optional documentation, listing, macro expansion and
absolute files, and can simulate the design with
.BR freecsim (1)
test vectors.
Its command line follows the compiler of WinCUPL 5.0a.
.PP
When
.I file
has no extension and does not exist, the extensions
.B .pld
and
.B .PLD
are tried.
.I device
is a device mnemonic and overrides the
.B Device
statement of the source.
.I library
is accepted and ignored; an argument is taken as the library when
.B \-u
is given, when there are three positional arguments, or when it ends
in
.BR .dl .
.PP
Single-letter flags may be combined behind one
.B \-
(for example
.BR \-jaxf ),
in either case.
.SH SERVE
.B freecupl serve
shows an editor page for the files of
.I directory
(default: the current one) in the web browser of the same machine:
editor with syntax colouring, Compile and Simulate with an options
dialog, messages that lead to the source line, viewers for the output
files and the device list.
The page is part of the program; nothing is loaded from the network.
The program listens on 127.0.0.1 only, prints the address of the page
with the token of the session, opens it in the default browser and
runs until it is interrupted or the Quit button of the page is used.
It runs
.B freecupl
and
.B freecsim
from its own directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " n"
TCP port; default: a free one.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-browser
Only print the address.
.PP
A source file called
.B serve
is compiled as
.BR "freecupl ./serve" .
docs/USERGUIDE.txt, section 9, describes the page.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
.B \-j
Write the JEDEC file
.IB NAME .jed\fR.
It is also written when none of
.B \-x \-l \-e \-a
is given.
.TP
.B \-x
Write the documentation file
.IB SOURCE .doc\fR.
.TP
.B \-f
Add the fuse plot to the documentation file.
.TP
.B \-l
Write the listing
.IB SOURCE .lst\fR.
.TP
.B \-e
Write the macro expansion
.IB SOURCE .mx\fR.
.TP
.B \-a
Write the absolute file
.IB SOURCE .abs
for
.BR freecsim (1).
.TP
.B \-s
Compile, write the absolute file and simulate
.IB SOURCE .si\fR,
writing
.IB SOURCE .so
and
.IB SOURCE .wo\fR;
with
.B \-j
the test vectors are appended to the JEDEC file.
.TP
.B \-n
Name the JEDEC file after the source file too, instead of the
.B Name
header item.
.TP
.BR \-m0 " ... " \-m4
Minimisation level: none, quick (the default,
.BR \-m1 ,
the sums of WinCUPL's default), and stronger levels that also remove
literals and may need fewer product terms than WinCUPL.
When a sum has too many product terms for its output, the design is
fitted once more with the shortest sums found (note 0909cb), at any
level but
.BR \-m0 ;
not with
.BR \-compat\-refdefect .
.TP
.B \-kb
For each output choose the polarity that needs fewer product terms.
.TP
.B \-g
Program the security fuse: the JEDEC file has G1 instead of G0
("Secure Device" in WinCUPL).
.TP
.B \-u
The next positional argument is the library (ignored).
.TP
.B \-w \-b \-h \-i \-p \-c \-d \-r \-o \-q \-kd \-ks \-kx
Accepted for compatibility; no effect.
.PP
.I SOURCE
is the source file's name without extension;
.I NAME
is taken from the
.B Name
header item as WinCUPL does it: up to the first blank, without a
directory or drive part, at most 28 characters
.RB ( no_name
when there is no item, the source file's name with
.BR \-n ).
Only the JEDEC file is named after it.
A name that is no file name on Windows (one of * ? " < > |, or a
device name such as CON or LPT1) is refused with message 0001cc on
every system.
Outputs are written next to the source file.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-compat\-refdefect
Reproduce WinCUPL 5.0a exactly, including its bugs, in the compiler and
in the simulator.
By default the bugs are corrected, with a warning where a design is
affected.
.TP
.B \-fix
Correct the bugs: the default.
Accepted for scripts written for FreeCUPL 1.0.0.
.TP
.B \-simlatch
With
.BR \-s :
simulate latched outputs and registered or latched inputs (timing not
verified); by default they show X.
.TP
.B \-devices
Print the supported device mnemonics and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I *.pld
CUPL source; files named by
.B $INCLUDE
are looked up in the current directory, then in the source's directory.
.TP
.I *.jed
JEDEC fuse file.
.TP
.I *.doc
Documentation: expanded product terms, symbol table, fuse plot.
.TP
.I *.lst
Listing with line numbers and messages.
.TP
.I *.mx
Source after the preprocessor.
.TP
.I *.abs
Compiled design for
.BR freecsim (1)
(FreeCUPL's own text format).
.TP
.IR *.si ", " *.so ", " *.wo
Simulation input, listing and waveform file.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FREECUPL_SERVE_INI
Settings file of
.BR "freecupl serve" ;
default
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/freecupl/serve.ini
or
.IR ~/.config/freecupl/serve.ini .
.TP
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
When set to a number of seconds since 1970-01-01 UTC, that time is
written as the creation date in the output headers instead of the
current time.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the outputs were written; 1 on errors, on an invalid flag, or
when the simulation started by
.B \-s
reported errors.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Messages have the form
.BI [ nnnnXX ]
.IR text ,
as in WinCUPL; codes 0901 to 0908 are FreeCUPL's notes about WinCUPL
behaviour.
Where a design meets a WinCUPL bug the message names it
.RB ( "bug #B001 fixed" ...).
FreeCUPL 1.0.0 reproduced the bugs by default.
docs/USERGUIDE.txt lists all codes.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
freecupl \-jxf counter.pld
freecupl \-jn g22v10 counter
freecupl \-jas counter
freecupl \-compat\-refdefect \-jx counter
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR freecsim (1),
.BR jedsim ,
.BR equiv ,
.BR jedtool ;
docs/USERGUIDE.txt and docs/BUGS.txt in the FreeCUPL distribution.
